  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "The new grab/interact changes in their current iteration are bad. They don't properly serve their function to eliminate accidental interactions/grabs and instead just making it incredibly hard to interact/grab when desired.\n\nWith the current simple half-sphere-like implementation which simply ignores any interactables when you pass the 180° mark (the direction you are pointing your hand) you will often miss grabbing or interacting with items as it is only natural to move your hands towards wanted interactions pointed at them, swiping past interactables (such as doors or keypads for instance) is also often prone to missing now as you often press the trigger while your hand is simply pointing at the object or inside the object.\n\nTwo suggestions to fix this.\n\nA: Options. Give us options to simply toggle this option off and/or better yet give us options to fully customize our interaction radius's and their priority with the ability to disable them separately if we desire. Options such as setting the angle, the size of our interaction/grab range, debug visualization to show us what it currently looks like, priority listing so we can set different priorities for front, pointing direction and back of our hand and of course toggles to turn them off, such as wanting the current implementation of turning the backside off.\n\nB: Improve the current implementation to make it less finicky, add a second small grab radius around our hand so we can still interact and grab with things that are directly in or on our hand regardless of whether they are behind our hand or not, this should drastically reduce the accidental misses and if you give things in front of the hand priority over things behind your hand, it should still serve its purpose of making it easier for us to choose what exactly we want to interact with. Although personally i'd prefer options here too, as i'd set the priority for the smaller interaction sphere higher than the front facing hand since i'd assume that i clip my hand into an item specifically to target said object over others around it.",
